<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/6th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · War</p><h1 class="enc-title">Vandalic War</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/533">533</a>–<a href="/years/534">534</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/6th-century">6th century</a> · <a href="/encyclopedia/wars">All wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Vandalic War was fought in 533–534 between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantine Empire</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/justinian-i">Justinian I</a> and the Vandal Kingdom in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-africa">North Africa</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belisarius">Belisarius</a> landed with a small army, defeated King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gelimer">Gelimer</a> at Ad Decimum and Tricamarum and took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carthage">Carthage</a>, restoring Roman rule in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a>; it was the first of Justinian's wars to recover the western provinces.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vandals">Vandals</a>, allied with the Alans, crossed the Straits of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gibraltar">Gibraltar</a> in May 429 at the call of the Roman governor Bonifacius, reportedly 80,000 in all. They took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carthage">Carthage</a> in October 439, and a treaty of 442 gave them the rich provinces of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zeugitana">Zeugitana</a> and Byzacena as their own possessions. Under King Geiseric their fleet raided across the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a> and sacked <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> in 455. Unlike other Germanic kingdoms, the Vandals replaced the Roman order rather than continuing it, and being adherents of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arianism">Arianism</a> they persecuted the Chalcedonian majority, especially under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/huneric">Huneric</a> and Gunthamund. Relations with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a> were often friendly under Thrasamund.</p><p>In 530 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gelimer">Gelimer</a> overthrew the pro-Roman king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hilderic">Hilderic</a> after a defeat by the local chief <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antalas">Antalas</a>. Justinian I took this as a pretext to intervene, once he had secured peace with Sassanid Persia in 532. Revolts in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sardinia">Sardinia</a>, under the governor Godas, and in Tripolitania, under Pudentius, distracted Gelimer, and he sent the bulk of the Vandal navy and army under his brother Tzazo to Sardinia.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belisarius">Belisarius</a>, whose secretary <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/procopius">Procopius</a> wrote the main narrative, sailed from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a> around 21 June 533. At Methone 500 men died of dysentery from badly baked bread. The army landed at Caputvada in early September, catching <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gelimer">Gelimer</a> by surprise. Belisarius presented himself as a liberator of the Latin-speaking population and punished soldiers who stole fruit, so that the army won the local people over. At Ad Decimum on 13 September Gelimer's plan to encircle the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> nearly succeeded, but Belisarius drove the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vandals">Vandals</a> into flight and entered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carthage">Carthage</a> on 15 September, where the citizens welcomed him. He sat on Gelimer's throne and ate the dinner that Gelimer had ordered, then set about repairing the walls.</p><p>In December Gelimer advanced from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulla-regia">Bulla Regia</a>, joined by Tzazo's army, and met the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> at Tricamarum, a cavalry battle in which Tzazo was killed. Gelimer fled to a remote mountain fortress, where he was blockaded until he surrendered in the spring.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>Officers hoping for advancement told Justinian that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belisarius">Belisarius</a> meant to set up his own kingdom, and Belisarius chose to return. He brought <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gelimer">Gelimer</a>, many captured <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vandals">Vandals</a>, who were enrolled in five regiments as the Vandals of Justinian, and the royal treasury, which included objects looted from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> eighty years earlier. He was given a triumph, the first in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a> since its foundation. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a> was restored to imperial rule as a praetorian prefecture, though control scarcely reached beyond the old kingdom. It was the first war of Justinian's attempt to reassert Roman sovereignty over the lost western provinces.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Vandalic War” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…the Vandals and Alans into Roman Africa and took Carthage in 439, held the grain lands of the empire and the western islands for 99 years, sacked Rome and defeated two imperial fleets, and persecuted the Nicene church until Belisarius destroyed it in the <b>Vandalic War</b> of 533–534.</blockquote><a href="/empire/vandal-kingdom">Vandal Kingdom · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li></ul></section>
